// prism_face -- Prism's component/view abstraction (PRISM_SPEC.md §5 face_decl, §12 reactivity's
// vocabulary, worked examples §19-21). core/, because widget/ builds NODES and prism_event.nova
// builds EVENTS, but nothing yet ties a name + state + a render function + an event-dispatch table
// together into one addressable unit -- which is what every worked example (§19 counter, §20
// task_list, §21 employee_form) actually IS.
//
// ── SCOPE, STATED HONESTLY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PRISM_SPEC.md's `face` is a LANGUAGE construct (§5 EBNF: `face_decl`) the compiler would parse,
// whose local `let` cells the compiler's §12 dependency-inference pass would track, and whose
// `->`/`when` actions the compiler would lower into typed dispatch. NONE of that parsing or
// inference exists yet -- `face`, `look`, `palette` are not NOVA keywords, and adding them is
// compiler-grammar work (RED blast radius; nova_compiler.nova is explicitly off-limits for this
// session, and is being actively edited elsewhere).
//
// What THIS file provides instead: the RUNTIME VALUE a compiled face_decl would eventually target.
// A face is representable, TODAY, in ordinary NOVA, as a named bundle of (a) a pure render function
// from state to a PrismNode tree, (b) a table of per-target event handlers, and (c) the four
// lifecycle hooks §5's closed `life_event` set names (show/hide/fail/load). An app can build one of
// these by hand -- see the KAT's `_counter_face`, a literal transcription of spec §19 -- and get
// real dispatch, real validation, and the same input-origin guarantee prism_event.nova already
// proved, without waiting for face syntax to exist.
//
// §12's REACTIVITY (automatic fine-grained invalidation: TEXT/PROP/ROW/SUBTREE/FACE granularity)
// is explicitly NOT implemented here. `prism_face_dispatch` returns the new state; the caller
// re-renders by calling `prism_face_render` again from scratch. That is always correct and always
// FACE-granularity -- §12.2's coarsest case, never wrong, never optimized. Building the dependency
// inference pass that picks a finer granularity is real compiler work (PRISM_STATUS.md M1.7), not
// silently pretended away here.
//
// ── WHY STATE IS A dict, NOT A COMPILER-GENERATED STRUCT ─────────────────────────────────────
// §12.1 defines a "cell" as a mutable `let` binding in the face body (`let n = 0`). Without a
// compiler that parses face bodies there is no per-face struct type to generate, so cells are
// represented as named entries in a `dict` -- the same escape valve prism_node.nova's `attrs` uses
// for "one generic value shape serves arbitrarily many call sites," and the exact shape
// forge_saga.nova already ships for a materially identical problem ("thread a mutable context
// through a sequence of named steps, each `fn(ctx: dict) -> Result<dict>`"). A real face's
// `let n = 0` becomes `state["n"] = 0` here. This is a library-level stand-in, not the final
// representation: the day face_decl compiles, cells become real typed locals and THIS dict layer
// is what its codegen would target, not what an app author writes directly forever.
//
// ── WHY A HANDLER RETURNS THE NEW STATE, NOT MUTATES IN PLACE ────────────────────────────────
// NOVA dicts mutate through a shared reference (`seen[key] = true` in prism_ui_pager.nova relies on
// exactly this), so an in-place-mutating handler signature (`fn(PrismEvent, dict) -> Result<()>`)
// would work today. Rejected anyway: "did the handler forget to touch state" is then
// indistinguishable from "the handler legitimately left state unchanged," and a KAT cannot tell the
// two apart by inspecting a return value that doesn't exist. `fn(PrismEvent, dict) -> Result<dict>`
// makes every handler's effect an explicit, assertable value -- the same reason
// prism_look.nova's `merge` returns a new look instead of mutating either input, and the same
// return shape forge_saga.nova's own step actions already use.
//
// ── FACE VALUES ARE BUILT FUNCTIONALLY: prism_face_on RETURNS A NEW PrismFace ────────────────
// Registering a handler or a hook copies the existing table into a fresh dict and adds one entry,
// rather than mutating `face.face_handlers` in place -- the identical discipline
// prism_look.prism_look_merge documents ("a base look is shared by every widget that uses it, so a
// mutating merge would let one caller's `.tight` silently restyle every other user of `card`").
// Here a `face` value returned by `prism_face_new` could in principle be handed to two different
// call sites that each want to attach their own handlers; a mutating `prism_face_on` would let the
// second caller's registration silently appear on the first caller's copy too.
//
// ── DISPATCH KEYS ON TARGET ALONE, NOT (TARGET, KIND) ────────────────────────────────────────
// prism_event.nova's six kinds are already 1:1 with the six interactive widgets (§9) -- a given
// target string names one control, which has exactly one kind. Keying the handler table on
// (target, kind) would let two DIFFERENT kinds silently share a target's handler slot, which cannot
// happen for a real widget tree; keying on target alone matches the real invariant instead of
// defending against a case the vocabulary already rules out.
//
// ── §15.7 INTEGRATION: `needs_user` IS DECLARATIVE, NOT INVENTED ─────────────────────────────
// §15.7: "security-relevant actions MAY require genuine user origin." prism_event.nova built the
// enforcement primitive (`prism_ev_require_user`) but left "which actions need it" to the caller.
// `prism_face_on(face, target, handler_fn, needs_user)` lets a registration DECLARE that
// requirement once, instead of every handler body remembering to call
// `prism_ev_require_user(ev)?` as its own first line -- dispatch enforces it centrally, so a
// forgotten call can no longer silently accept a synthetic/replayed event for that target. This is
// §15.7 applied, not a new rule: the spec already names the property; this is where a face-level
// API for it lives.
//
// ── HOOKS: show/hide/load SHARE A SIGNATURE, fail DOES NOT ───────────────────────────────────
// §5's `life_event` is the closed set {show, hide, fail, load}. show/hide/load hooks are
// `fn(dict) -> Result<dict>` (state in, state out) -- a face becoming visible, hidden, or finishing
// an async load is a pure state transition. `fail` additionally carries the failure message (worked
// example §21: `on fail -> label "Could not save: {it.message}"`), so its hook is
// `fn(dict, string) -> Result<dict>`. Both live in the same `face_hooks: dict` (keyed by fixed
// internal name), which is safe only because callers never invoke a hook generically -- each of
// `prism_face_show/_hide/_load/_fail` calls its OWN named slot with its OWN fixed arity, so a
// show hook can never be accidentally invoked with a fail hook's two-argument shape.
//
// ⚠ NOT MODELLED, DELIBERATELY: §5's node_body-scoped inline form (`on fail -> label "..."` written
// INSIDE a node body, as a per-subtree rendering fallback rather than a face-level state hook) is a
// DIFFERENT feature -- an error-boundary that swaps in fallback UI for one subtree -- and needs
// integration with widget/ tree construction that does not exist yet. Only the face-body-level
// lifecycle hook (a state transition, run via `prism_face_fail`) is implemented here. Recorded so
// this is not mistaken for full §21 fidelity.
//
// ⚠ NOT ENFORCED: nothing stops app code from calling a handler or hook closure directly, bypassing
// `prism_face_dispatch` and its §15.7 gate -- the identical CONVENTIONAL-not-structural limitation
// prism_event.nova already states for `*_from_input`. The guarantee holds as long as dispatch is
// the only call path exercised, and becomes structural once the compositor (§15.8) is the sole
// producer of events and the sole caller of dispatch.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   PrismFace                                                     the value type
//   prism_face_new(name, render_fn)                    -> Result<PrismFace>
//     render_fn: fn(dict) -> Result<PrismNode>. No handlers/hooks yet.
//   prism_face_on(face, target, handler_fn, needs_user: bool) -> Result<PrismFace>
//     handler_fn: fn(PrismEvent, dict) -> Result<dict>. Rejects an empty/duplicate target.
//   prism_face_on_show/_hide/_load(face, hook_fn)     -> Result<PrismFace>  fn(dict) -> Result<dict>
//   prism_face_on_fail(face, hook_fn)                 -> Result<PrismFace>  fn(dict, string) -> Result<dict>
//     (each rejects re-registering the same hook)
//   prism_face_name(face)                              -> string
//   prism_face_has_handler(face, target)               -> bool
//   prism_face_handler_count(face)                      -> int
//   prism_face_handler_needs_user(face, target)         -> Result<bool>
//   prism_face_has_hook(face, name)                     -> bool   name in {"show","hide","load","fail"}
//   prism_face_render(face, state)                      -> Result<PrismNode>
//   prism_face_dispatch(face, event, state)             -> Result<dict>
//     Looks up event's target, enforces §15.7 if that handler needs_user, runs it.
//   prism_face_show/_hide/_load(face, state)            -> Result<dict>  runs that hook if present,
//     else `ok(state)` unchanged -- a no-op, not an error (not every face cares about a life event).
//   prism_face_fail(face, state, message)               -> Result<dict>  same no-op-if-absent rule.

import prism_node
import prism_event

// The runtime value a compiled face_decl would target. `face_render` and the closures stored
// inside `face_handlers`/`face_hooks` are typed `any` -- NOVA closures are stored on struct fields
// this way elsewhere in the codebase (forge.nova's `WsConn.recv_fn: any` / `send_fn: any`,
// prism_render_canvas.nova's `pcv_measure: any`), and every call site here extracts the closure
// into a local before calling it (`let h = ...; h(...)`), matching forge_saga.nova's own
// `let act = step[1]` discipline -- the verified-safe shape for calling a closure that came out of
// a dynamically-typed slot.
type PrismFace
    face_name: string
    face_render: any
    face_handlers: dict   // target(string) -> [handler_fn(any), needs_user_flag(int 0/1)]
    face_hooks: dict      // "show"|"hide"|"load"|"fail" -> hook_fn(any)

// ── Construction ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn prism_face_new(name: string, render_fn) -> Result<PrismFace>
    if len(name) == 0
        return err("prism_face: name must not be empty")
    ok(PrismFace(name, render_fn, {}, {}))

// Register one target's handler. Returns a NEW face (see header) with a duplicate check: two
// handlers silently sharing a target is the same mistake `each`/`grid`'s mandatory-unique `by` and
// prism_pg_size_select's duplicate-size check exist to catch one layer over.
fn prism_face_on(face: PrismFace, target: string, handler_fn, needs_user: bool) -> Result<PrismFace>
    if len(target) == 0
        return err("prism_face: target must not be empty")
    if target in face.face_handlers
        return err("prism_face: a handler is already registered for target '{target}' on face '{face.face_name}'")
    let next = {}
    for k in face.face_handlers
        next[k] = face.face_handlers[k]
    let flag = 1 if needs_user else 0
    next[target] = [handler_fn, flag]
    ok(PrismFace(face.face_name, face.face_render, next, face.face_hooks))

fn _prism_face_set_hook(face: PrismFace, name: string, hook_fn) -> Result<PrismFace>
    if name in face.face_hooks
        return err("prism_face: a '{name}' hook is already registered on face '{face.face_name}'")
    let next = {}
    for k in face.face_hooks
        next[k] = face.face_hooks[k]
    next[name] = hook_fn
    ok(PrismFace(face.face_name, face.face_render, face.face_handlers, next))

// show/hide/load: fn(dict) -> Result<dict>
fn prism_face_on_show(face: PrismFace, hook_fn) -> Result<PrismFace>
    _prism_face_set_hook(face, "show", hook_fn)

fn prism_face_on_hide(face: PrismFace, hook_fn) -> Result<PrismFace>
    _prism_face_set_hook(face, "hide", hook_fn)

fn prism_face_on_load(face: PrismFace, hook_fn) -> Result<PrismFace>
    _prism_face_set_hook(face, "load", hook_fn)

// fail: fn(dict, string) -> Result<dict> -- see header for why this one's arity differs.
fn prism_face_on_fail(face: PrismFace, hook_fn) -> Result<PrismFace>
    _prism_face_set_hook(face, "fail", hook_fn)

// ── Inspection ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn prism_face_name(face: PrismFace) -> string
    face.face_name

fn prism_face_has_handler(face: PrismFace, target: string) -> bool
    target in face.face_handlers

fn prism_face_handler_count(face: PrismFace) -> int
    len(face.face_handlers)

fn prism_face_handler_needs_user(face: PrismFace, target: string) -> Result<bool>
    if target not in face.face_handlers
        return err("prism_face: no handler registered for target '{target}' on face '{face.face_name}'")
    let entry = face.face_handlers[target]
    ok(entry[1] == 1)

fn prism_face_has_hook(face: PrismFace, name: string) -> bool
    name in face.face_hooks

// ── Render ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn prism_face_render(face: PrismFace, state: dict) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let render = face.face_render
    render(state)

// ── Dispatch (the §15.7 gate lives here) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

// NOTE: `prism_ev_require_user` returns `Result<PrismEvent>`, a DIFFERENT Ok-type parameter than
// this function's `Result<dict>` -- using `?` across that mismatch is the exact E1001 type-mismatch
// class prism_ui_kit.prism_ui_collect's own header documents ("returning the element's Result
// straight out unifies the two type parameters"). So this checks `is_err` explicitly and re-wraps
// the message, the same one-line fix prism_ui_collect already uses for the identical shape.
fn prism_face_dispatch(face: PrismFace, event: PrismEvent, state: dict) -> Result<dict>
    let target = prism_event.prism_ev_target(event)
    if target not in face.face_handlers
        return err("prism_face: no handler registered for target '{target}' on face '{face.face_name}'")
    let entry = face.face_handlers[target]
    let needs_user = entry[1]
    if needs_user == 1
        let gate = prism_event.prism_ev_require_user(event)
        if is_err(gate)
            return err(unwrap_err(gate))
    let handler = entry[0]
    handler(event, state)

// ── Lifecycle runners: no-op (state unchanged) if the hook was never registered ──────────────

fn prism_face_show(face: PrismFace, state: dict) -> Result<dict>
    if "show" not in face.face_hooks
        return ok(state)
    let hook = face.face_hooks["show"]
    hook(state)

fn prism_face_hide(face: PrismFace, state: dict) -> Result<dict>
    if "hide" not in face.face_hooks
        return ok(state)
    let hook = face.face_hooks["hide"]
    hook(state)

fn prism_face_load(face: PrismFace, state: dict) -> Result<dict>
    if "load" not in face.face_hooks
        return ok(state)
    let hook = face.face_hooks["load"]
    hook(state)

fn prism_face_fail(face: PrismFace, state: dict, message: string) -> Result<dict>
    if "fail" not in face.face_hooks
        return ok(state)
    let hook = face.face_hooks["fail"]
    hook(state, message)
